  "reasoning": "The patient presents with a cluster of systemic symptoms: fever, headache, swollen lymph nodes, weakness, and fatigue. Difficulty concentrating is attributed to the fever. Evaluating against the provided list: there are no localized respiratory symptoms (cough, congestion, shortness of breath) to suggest common cold, pneumonia, or bronchial asthma. There is no prominent gastrointestinal involvement (abdominal pain, vomiting, diarrhea/constipation) to point toward typhoid, peptic ulcer disease, GERD, or UTI. No characteristic skin rashes, peeling, or intense itching are reported, ruling out chicken pox, psoriasis, fungal infection, impetigo, and drug reaction. No specific joint swelling or chronic back/neck stiffness is noted, excluding arthritis and cervical spondylosis. The core presentation of fever, headache, and significant systemic weakness/fatigue strongly aligns with malaria, which is a systemic parasitic infection known for causing these exact constitutional symptoms. Swollen lymph nodes are a common immune response in such infections. Therefore, malaria is the most fitting diagnosis.",
  "final_answer": "[DIAGNOSIS]malaria[/DIAGNOSIS]"
